{"hash":"2ad1f3561f5ba00d","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":96.85,"said":"Here is the Amrit Kund, the temple’s old stone tank, still and deep. Water is the heart of this place, so let me tell you the story the Shiva Purana tells, of a time when there was none. Long ago the sage Gautama lived on Brahmagiri with his wife Ahalya, a woman of great virtue. Then came a drought that lasted, the Purana says, a hundred years. Not one green shoot was left on the earth. People, animals and birds fled. Gautama sat for six months in penance, worshipping Varuna, lord of the waters, until Varuna appeared before him. Gautama asked for rain. ‘I cannot overrule the will of the gods,’ said Varuna. ‘Ask for something else.’ So Gautama asked for water that would never fail. ‘Dig a pit,’ said Varuna. Gautama dug one, just a hand deep, and Varuna filled it with divine water that would never run dry. Fields grew up around it. Grain, flowers and fruit returned, and thousands of sages came to live there with their families, thanks to Gautama. But not everyone was grateful. One morning, at that very water, a quarrel began. Walk with me to Nandi, and I will tell you where it led.\n\nDid you know? In 2026, while the Archaeological Survey of India was desilting the Amrit Kund, also called Amritavarshini, an old Shiva linga was found at its bottom, about 65 feet down.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1096},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1112,"end":1283}],"words":[[0.098,0.397,0,4],[0.507,0.096,5,7],[0.615,0.168,8,11],[0.795,0.397,12,17],[1.204,0.373,18,22],[1.805,0.108,24,27],[1.925,0.577,28,36],[2.514,0.252,37,40],[2.778,0.373,41,46],[3.163,0.445,47,51],[3.8,0.385,53,58],[4.197,0.3,59,62],[4.509,0.373,63,67],[5.805,0.373,69,74],[6.19,0.144,75,77],[6.346,0.108,78,81],[6.466,0.288,82,87],[6.767,0.096,88,90],[6.875,0.204,91,95],[7.091,0.445,96,101],[7.801,0.18,103,105],[7.993,0.24,106,109],[8.245,0.096,110,112],[8.353,0.276,113,117],[8.642,0.132,118,121],[8.786,0.108